plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: plato-republic-1.logic.r2 and plato-republic-1.logic.r2.2 have "unjust_seeming → fare_well" and "(unjust_seeming → fare_well) → ¬reason_choose_justice"; plato-republic-1.logic.r2.3 has "unjust_seeming → fare_well"; they differ when only "one unites injustice with a deceitful regard to appearances", "one fares to one's mind with gods and men, in life and after death" and "there is a principle on which to choose justice rather than the worst injustice" are true, which the second allows
plato-republic-1.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps plato-republic-1.logic.r2 and plato-republic-1.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "one unites injustice with a deceitful regard to appearances", "one fares to one's mind with gods and men, in life and after death" and "there is a principle on which to choose justice rather than the worst injustice" are true. Is S1, "On what principle, then, shall we any longer choose justice rather than the worst injustice? when, if we only unite the latter with a deceitful regard to appearances, we shall fare to our mind both with gods and men, in life and after death, as the most numerous and the highest authorities tell us.", true then?
plato-republic-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: plato-republic-1.logic.r2 and plato-republic-1.logic.r2.2 have "¬reason_choose_justice → (superior → ¬honour_justice)"; plato-republic-1.logic.r2.3 has "(unjust_seeming → fare_well) → ¬reason_choose_justice" and "¬reason_choose_justice → (superior → ¬honour_justice)" and "(obtains_power → becomes_unjust) → (blames_injustice → lacks_power)"; they differ when only "one unites injustice with a deceitful regard to appearances", "one fares to one's mind with gods and men, in life and after death" and "there is a principle on which to choose justice rather than the worst injustice" are true, which the first allows
plato-republic-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: plato-republic-1.logic.r2.3 takes for granted "if (if he obtains the power of being unjust, then he immediately becomes unjust as far as he can be), then (if a man blames injustice, then owing to cowardice or age or some weakness, he has not the power of being unjust)"; plato-republic-1.logic.r2 and plato-republic-1.logic.r2.2 do not
plato-republic-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps plato-republic-1.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (if he obtains the power of being unjust, then he immediately becomes unjust as far as he can be), then (if a man blames injustice, then owing to cowardice or age or some weakness, he has not the power of being unjust)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
